--- A(iv) the two (SEC-RANK) discharges --------------------------------------
Level one. The quoted HE3 sentence is HE3_PROOF:1167-1169, inside HE3-A-PROOF
(span 1092-1205, both delimiters unique — §B). Read in context (the *Stage-α
step*, HE3:1149-1169): the step fires exactly "If the read refines at (λ, s) (a
residual root of multiplicity m ≥ 2 ...)" — i.e. at deg r' = 1 with integer
slope, which is the master's product-1 linear recentering. The termination
clause "each refine strictly increases the floor λ and dv-heights are bounded by
the leaf's window (GENHN-3(a)), so the chain is finite" does give an N-valued
sigma (window bound minus floor), decreasing at exactly that transition and at
no other requirement. The master's characterisation is FAITHFUL.  CLEAN.

Level two. HE7-8 (HE7_PROOF:1087-1128) is a well-foundedness statement, not a
rank: "suppose the read performs an infinite chain of α-refines ... Then f_S is
a perfect μ₂-th power in O[x] — contradicting disc f_S ≠ 0. Hence every
α-refine chain is finite." I checked the logical adequacy of that form for
(SEC-RANK): a relation with no infinite descending chain admits an ordinal rank
function, so "no infinite chain of consecutive recenterings" does yield a
well-founded W_C with sigma_C strictly decreasing at recenterings — which is
why (SEC-RANK) is correctly stated with W_C merely well-founded. I also checked
the hypothesis fit: HE7-8 needs the node polygon to be "the single side of
length μ₂ with residual (Z − s_j)^{μ₂}", and the master's split-first discipline
(r2 repair 8) guarantees a recentering is only ever taken at a single-side pure
label block, where (RES-DEG) with e'=1 forces deg R = L = mu and R = c(Y-s)^mu.
The fit is exact and non-obvious; it holds.  CLEAN as a level-two statement.
What is NOT clean is its transport to the composed keys of S3.4/S3.5 — see
finding G-1 — and its use as the recentered-key peel discharge — finding G-2.

